{
  "id": "Compound::DB00692 - Gene::152",
  "verbalization": "Phentolamine, a competitive antagonist of alpha-adrenergic receptors, appears to interact with Adra1d, a subunit of the alpha-adrenergic receptor, through a series of interconnected pathways. The evidence suggests that Phentolamine binds to Adra1d, which then participates in the broader category of \"Alpha-adrenergic Receptor Activity\" or \"Adrenergic Receptor Activity,\" both of which are ultimately participated by Adra2c. This implies a potential functional link between Phentolamine and Adra2c, where the activation or modulation of Adra1d may influence the activity of Adra2c through shared receptor pathways. The consistent binding of Phentolamine to Adra1d across all three paths, coupled with the shared participation in receptor activity, suggests a mechanism by which Phentolamine could modulate Adra2c function. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of Adra1d as a bridge between Phentolamine and Adra2c, emphasizing the importance of receptor subunit interactions in determining drug-target relationships.",
